    "turn": 1,
    "user": "Can you explain what a Jacobian is, in a few sentences?",
    "response": "The Jacobian is a matrix of all first-order partial derivatives of a vector-valued function. It describes how a function transforms small changes in input variables into changes in output variables. In multivariable calculus, the Jacobian matrix is used to analyze the local behavior of functions, and its determinant (the Jacobian determinant) is important in change of variables in integration and in determining whether a function is locally invertible.",
